 # Shadow Volume Rendering
 
-Also called: Volumetric Shadow Maps
+Also called: Lighting Shadow Volume, Precomputed Shadow Map for Volumes, Shadow Volume Computation, Volumetric Shadow Maps
 
 ## Overview
-Shadow Volume Rendering is a specialized ray marching routine that computes only the accumulated alpha (transmittance) along a ray to generate shadow maps or occlusion values for volume scenes [S1, unofficial]. It precomputes a shadow volume to store the amount of light arriving at each sample after attenuation by intervening material [S2]. It differs from [Ray Marching with Shadow Mapping](1415-ray-marching-with-shadow-mapping.md) by explicitly precomputing a shadow volume structure rather than relying solely on runtime opacity shadow maps, and from [Volume Shadow Calculation via Light Strength Buffer](1914-volume-shadow-calculation-via-light-strength-buffer.md) by focusing on alpha accumulation for shadow generation rather than light strength buffering [S2].
+Shadow Volume Rendering computes lighting shadows for volume rendering by precomputing a 3D texture (the shadow volume) that stores the accumulated opacity (transmittance) along rays cast from each voxel toward the light source [S1, unofficial][S3]. This allows the renderer to sample the shadow volume during the main volume integration pass to determine self-occlusion, avoiding the per-sample ray marching cost of calculating shadow at render time [S3]. It differs from [Ray Marching with Shadow Mapping](1415-ray-marching-with-shadow-mapping.md) by decoupling shadow calculation from the view-dependent ray march, trading memory bandwidth for reduced compute during the final image pass [S1, unofficial]. It is distinct from [Volume Shadow Calculation via Light Strength Buffer](1914-volume-shadow-calculation-via-light-strength-buffer.md) which accumulates light attenuation in a screen-space buffer, and [Deep Shadow Maps](2166-deep-shadow-maps.md) which store transmittance distributions in 2D textures rather than a 3D volume [S3].
 
 ## How it works
+The method consists of two phases: precomputing the shadow volume using a compute shader, and sampling it during the final volume rendering pass.
+
+### Inputs and Outputs
 **Inputs:**
-- Ray origin `org_world` and direction `dir_world` in world space [S1, unofficial].
-- World-to-object transformation matrix `M` [S1, unofficial].
-- Ray bounds `t_min` and `t_max` [S1, unofficial].
-- Base step size `step` [S1, unofficial].
-- Sampling scale multiplier `sampling_scale` [S1, unofficial].
-- Random number generator state `rng` (e.g., `RandomTEA`) [S1, unofficial].
+*   `_VolumeTexture`: `Texture3D` containing the density field of the participating medium [S1, unofficial].
+*   `_TFTex`: `Texture2D` containing the 1D transfer function (TF), mapping density to color/alpha [S1, unofficial].
+*   `_LightDirection`: `float3` defining the direction of the light source (assumed directional for the basic implementation) [S1, unofficial].
+*   `_Dimension`: `int3` specifying the resolution of the output shadow volume [S1, unofficial].
+*   `_MinVal`, `_MaxVal`: `float` thresholds for density culling [S1, unofficial].
+*   `_TextureSize`: `float3` resolution of the input volume texture [S1, unofficial].
+*   `numSteps`: Integer, fixed at 32 in the reference implementation [S1, unofficial].
+*   `stepSize`: Float, calculated as $0.25 / \text{numSteps}$ (typically $0.0078125$) [S1, unofficial].
 
 **Outputs:**
-- Accumulated alpha (transmittance) value [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Data Structures:**
-- `ShadowPayload`:
-  - `rng`: Pointer to `RandomTEA` instance [S1, unofficial].
-  - `alpha`: Float, accumulated alpha [S1, unofficial].
-  - Layout: Packaged via `packPointer(&payload, u0, u1)` [S1, unofficial].
